# Model Context Protocol (MCP) Assurance Report
**Generated:** <normalized-timestamp>

## Reader Key: Terms And Abbreviations

| Term | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- |
| **MCP** | Model Context Protocol, the communication protocol evaluated by this report. |
| **MCP specification profile** | The dated MCP rules and schema version selected for validation. |
| **Priority 1-4** | Remediation dependency order: Priority 1 should be addressed first and Priority 4 later. Priority is not severity. |
| **Certificate eligibility** | A deterministic decision that states whether every requirement of the selected assurance profile is satisfied. |
| **Typed claim** | A conclusion bound to authority, requirement level, applicability, confidence, and immutable evidence references. |
| **Independent assessments** | Core conformance, security assurance, and operational readiness are reduced separately and never averaged into one score. |
| **Authority labels** | Specification means a protocol requirement; Guideline means recommended practice; Heuristic means deterministic advisory analysis; Operational means runtime or infrastructure evidence. |
| **JSON-RPC / HTTP** | JSON-RPC is JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) for remote procedure call (RPC) messages used by MCP. HTTP is the Hypertext Transfer Protocol used to transport messages in this run. |
| **RFC 2119: MUST / SHOULD / MAY** | Requirement strength: MUST is required, SHOULD is recommended unless a justified exception exists, and MAY is optional. |
| **P50 / P95 / P99** | Latency percentiles: 50%, 95%, or 99% of measured requests completed at or below the reported value. |
| **Units and time** | ms means milliseconds, req/s means requests per second, and UTC means Coordinated Universal Time. |
| **AI / LLM / CLI / UX** | Artificial intelligence; large language model; command-line interface; and user experience. |
| **VS Code** | Visual Studio Code, a supported client profile named in compatibility results. |
| **Identifiers: ID / UUID** | ID means identifier. UUID means Universally Unique Identifier. Validation and rule identifiers are stable machine references, not scores. |

